@ Index:   Commodore 64 sound tricks on the buzzer
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 1
@ Tested:  on the calculator: the three parts in turn, about 15 s (8.6 s of chords, 3 s of lead, 3.4 s plucked)
@ Input:   none
@ Output:  sound only; the stack cleared, your buzzer volume given back
@
@ The C64's SID chip had 3 voices, 4 waveforms, ADSR envelopes and a filter. The C47 has ONE buzzer that plays
@ one square-ish note at a time. C64 musicians used three tricks to sound richer than the hardware; this demo
@ plays all three, in A minor (an original tune):
@   1. Fake chords (arpeggio): the three notes of a chord in turn, 30 ms each, so fast that the ear hears
@      one chord.
@   2. Vibrato: one long note cut into 25 ms pieces whose pitch goes up and down 1.2 % along a sine
@      (about 6.7 times a second).
@   3. Envelope: one note cut into 5 pieces of 40 ms at volume 11 8 6 4 2, so it sounds plucked
@      (loud attack, fading tail).
@
@ Sound commands (C47 firmware, c47-dmcp/hal/audio.c):
@   BUZZ   Y = frequency in Hz (whole Hz, 0 = silence), X = length in ms (max 2000); the program waits
@          until the note ends. The stack is unchanged.
@   VOL n  buzzer volume 0-11; VOL# puts the current volume in X; VOL IND "KVOL" sets it from a variable
@          (used here to give yours back).
@   TONE n / BEEP play fixed 250 ms notes (not used here).
@ The PC simulator plays TONE and BEEP only: BUZZ is silent there, so run C64FX on the calculator.
@
@ Notes are given as semitones from A4 (440 Hz): 0 = A4, 3 = C5, 7 = E5, -4 = F4 ...
@ LBL 05 turns them into Hz: f = 440 x 2^(s/12).
@ Uses local registers only (LocR) and the variable KVOL (deleted at the end).
@ Side effect: the angle mode is set to DEG (for the vibrato's SIN).
@ One program, C64FX its only global label; the loops are LBL / DSE / GTO (no STRUCT commands needed).
@
LBL "C64FX"
  # ---- main: your volume kept, the three parts in turn, then your volume back
  VOL#
  STO "KVOL"
  DEG
  # ---- PART 1: fake chords Am F G E Am F Dm E (Z Y X = the chord's three semitones from A4)
  0
  3
  7
  # Am: A4 C5 E5
  XEQ 01
  -4
  0
  3
  # F: F4 A4 C5
  XEQ 01
  -2
  2
  5
  # G: G4 B4 D5
  XEQ 01
  -5
  -1
  2
  # E: E4 G#4 B4
  XEQ 01
  0
  3
  7
  # Am
  XEQ 01
  -4
  0
  3
  # F
  XEQ 01
  -7
  -3
  0
  # Dm: D4 F4 A4
  XEQ 01
  -5
  -1
  2
  # E
  XEQ 01
  # ---- PART 2: the lead, 9 notes with vibrato (Y = length in ms, X = semitone)
  300
  0
  # A4
  XEQ 03
  150
  3
  # C5
  XEQ 03
  150
  2
  # B4
  XEQ 03
  300
  0
  # A4
  XEQ 03
  600
  -5
  # E4
  XEQ 03
  300
  -4
  # F4
  XEQ 03
  150
  -5
  # E4
  XEQ 03
  150
  -7
  # D4
  XEQ 03
  900
  -5
  # E4, the long one
  XEQ 03
  # ---- PART 3: 17 plucked notes (a broken-chord line), 200 ms each
  0
  XEQ 04
  7
  XEQ 04
  12
  XEQ 04
  7
  XEQ 04
  3
  XEQ 04
  7
  XEQ 04
  12
  XEQ 04
  15
  XEQ 04
  -4
  XEQ 04
  3
  XEQ 04
  8
  XEQ 04
  3
  XEQ 04
  -5
  XEQ 04
  -1
  XEQ 04
  2
  XEQ 04
  7
  XEQ 04
  0
  XEQ 04
  # ---- the end: your volume back, KVOL deleted, the stack cleared
  VOL IND "KVOL"
  DELITM "KVOL"
  CLSTK
  RTN

LBL 01
  # ---- FAKE CHORD. In: Z Y X = three semitones. 12 rounds of the three notes,
  # ---- 30 ms each = 1.08 s of one chord (36 notes)
  # R.00 R.01 R.02 the three frequencies, R.03 the round counter
  LocR 04
  XEQ 05
  # X's note in Hz
  STO R.00
  R↓
  XEQ 05
  # Y's note
  STO R.01
  R↓
  XEQ 05
  # Z's note
  STO R.02
  12
  # 12 rounds
  STO R.03
  # the round loop: three notes, then DSE
  LBL 02
    RCL R.02
    30
    # lowest note, 30 ms
    BUZZ
    RCL R.01
    30
    # middle note
    BUZZ
    RCL R.00
    30
    # top note
    BUZZ
    CLSTK
    # one round less; DSE skips the GTO when no round is left
    DSE R.03
    GTO 02
  RTN

LBL 03
  # ---- VIBRATO. In: Y = length in ms, X = semitone. The note is cut into ms/25 pieces of 25 ms;
  # ---- piece k has pitch f x (1 + 0.012 sin(60k)): a full wobble every 6 pieces (150 ms = 6.7 Hz),
  # ---- +-1.2 % = +-20 cents
  # R.00 the frequency, R.01 the pieces left, R.02 k
  LocR 03
  XEQ 05
  STO R.00
  R↓
  25
  ÷
  IP
  # ms / 25 pieces
  STO R.01
  0
  # k = 0
  STO R.02
  # the piece loop: one 25 ms piece, then DSE
  LBL 06
    RCL R.02
    60
    ×
    SIN
    0.012
    ×
    1
    +
    RCL R.00
    # f x (1 + 0.012 sin(60k))
    ×
    25
    # one piece, 25 ms
    BUZZ
    CLSTK
    1
    # k + 1
    STO+ R.02
    # one piece less; DSE skips the GTO when no piece is left
    DSE R.01
    GTO 06
  RTN

LBL 04
  # ---- PLUCK (volume envelope). In: X = semitone. Five pieces of 40 ms with the volume
  # ---- stepping down 11 8 6 4 2 (200 ms a note); unrolled, one piece after the other
  # R.00 the frequency
  LocR 01
  XEQ 05
  STO R.00
  # attack
  VOL 11
  RCL R.00
  40
  BUZZ
  CLSTK
  VOL 8
  RCL R.00
  40
  BUZZ
  CLSTK
  VOL 6
  RCL R.00
  40
  BUZZ
  CLSTK
  VOL 4
  RCL R.00
  40
  BUZZ
  CLSTK
  # the fading tail
  VOL 2
  RCL R.00
  40
  BUZZ
  CLSTK
  RTN

LBL 05
  # ---- SEMITONES -> Hz. X = s from A4  ->  X = 440 x 2^(s/12)
  12
  ÷
  2ˣ
  440
  ×
  RTN
END
